I choose SQL when the application has structured and relational data where relationships, transactions, and strong consistency 
are important. For example, in an appointment booking system, users, doctors, appointments, and payments have clear relationships, 
so I would prefer PostgreSQL or MySQL.
I choose MongoDB when the data is more flexible or changes frequently, and I need a document-based model with 
easy horizontal scaling. For example, product catalogs where different products can have different attributes are a
good use case for MongoDB.
So, my choice depends on the data structure, relationships, transaction requirements, scalability, and query patterns, 
rather than simply choosing one database over the other.





